Ever stared at a rapid-fire wand and wondered what actually fires together each cast? This mod draws your wand's cast structure right onto the inventory UI, Lisp/SLIME style, so the grouping is visible instead of guessed.

[h2]Two views[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Slot brackets[/b] — rainbow nesting brackets on the spell cards themselves, hugging each group's first and last card. Colors cycle by nesting depth, like nested code brackets: you can see what a multicast gathers, which modifiers feed which projectile, what payload a trigger opens, and — one bracket further out — exactly which spells fire together in each cast.
[*][b]Wand structure panel[/b] — an indented text tree of the selected wand, one section per cast, spell names colored by type. Always-cast spells are listed separately.
[/list]
Both update live as you drag spells around, so you can see a wand reshape itself while you build it.

[h2]It shows you the WRAP[/h2]
The one that actually costs you damage: when a cast runs past the last spell in the wand, the draw wraps back to the front and forces the recharge. That cast fires a mix you probably didn't intend, and nothing in the vanilla UI tells you it's happening.

The wrap gets marked in orange — one bracket enclosing the whole looping structure, from the first card the wrap pulled back in to where the cast ran out of deck, tagged [i]wraps to front[/i] at its closing end. The panel gets a loud [b]WRAPS! -> recharge[/b] banner on that cast.

[h2]New in v1.3.3 — Divide By fixed, and honest question marks[/h2]
[b]Divide By spells now group correctly.[/b] Divide By 2/3/4/10 skip the engine's normal draw call and invoke the next card directly, which the mod's spell data missed — so it drew a cast boundary between a divide and its target. In the game they fire [i]together[/i]: [code]Divide By 2, Light Bullet[/code] is one cast, and a multicast that gathers a divide spans the divide [i]and[/i] what it divides. The mod now attaches a Divide By to the next spell exactly like a modifier prefix — with the one real difference kept: a Divide By at the end of the wand does nothing at all, so it never causes a wrap (a trailing modifier does).

[b]Spells the mod can't predict now say so.[/b] The Requirement ("If") spells skip part of the wand when their condition is false at cast time, and the random-draw spells (Random Spell, Draw Random, …) add cards chosen at cast time. No fixed display can show those truthfully, so instead of silently drawing one possibility as if it were certain, the panel marks them with a [b]?[/b] and a footnote. What's drawn is the condition-true / no-extras path.

If you hit a wand whose brackets don't match what it actually does, please post the spell list (or a screenshot) — every confirmed mismatch so far has been fixable, and this update exists because people reported them.

[h2]v1.3.2 — casts, and a rebuilt wrap[/h2]
[b]Casts are bracketed now.[/b] Which spells fire [i]together[/i] is the thing the mod exists to show, and until now you had to read it off the panel. Each cast gets its own bracket, one level outside the spell groups it contains, and the rainbow runs as a single progression across both axes — advancing per cast and per nesting level — so the colors keep telling you where you are. A cast that fires a single spell isn't bracketed: there's no simultaneity to show, and the pair would just be ink.

[b]The wrap reads as a loop.[/b] It used to be drawn as two separate orange bracket pairs joined by a line under the row, which looked like two unrelated groups rather than one structure continuing around. It's now a single enclosing bracket around everything the wrap touches:

[code][ chainsaw, chainsaw, [Double spell, Spitter [Double spell, Spitter]] ][/code]

Also fixed: brackets sharing a card edge used to blur into plain vertical bars, because an outer bracket's hooks were drawn underneath the one nested inside it. And a bare modifier chain that wrapped off the end of the deck drew no wrap marking at all — that case is covered now.

[h2]v1.3.1 — font compatibility[/h2]
If you use a font mod (such as [i]Better Font (English)[/i]) or play in a language with a smooth, non-pixelated font (Chinese, Japanese, …), the wand structure panel used to render squished against the right edge of the screen with its text running off it. That's fixed.

The cause: with those fonts Noita ignores the smaller text sizes when [i]drawing[/i], while still applying them when [i]measuring[/i] — so the panel was built around three times too small for the text that landed in it. The panel now detects this and sizes itself to the one setting those fonts render faithfully.

[b]With one of those fonts, Text Size will stay on Large and won't change.[/b] That's deliberate, not a broken setting — it's the only size that renders correctly. On the default pixel font nothing changes at all and every size works exactly as before.

Also in this update:
[list]
[*]A new [b]Large[/b] text size for the structure panel.
[*]Fixed truncation of non-English spell names, which could cut a character in half and leave a garbled glyph behind.
[*]The Debug Info box now reports what your font is doing, so one screenshot is enough to pin down a font problem.
[/list]
Thanks to [b]BourbonCrow[/b] and [b]shinylavawarrior[/b] for reporting this — and to BourbonCrow for spotting that a font mod was the trigger, which is what made it findable at all.

[h2]v1.3.0 — depleted spells[/h2]
A spell with 0 charges left can't fire: the engine discards it and draws the next card instead. So the mod now leaves depleted spells out of the structure entirely, and your wand groups — and [b]wraps[/b] — as if that slot were empty. A single spent spell can move the wrap point, which is exactly the kind of thing that's invisible mid-run.

One exception, on by default: the Greek spells (Alpha, Gamma, Tau, Omega, Mu, Phi, Sigma, Zeta) re-cast spells by their [i]position[/i] in the wand, so a depleted spell still changes what they pick up. On a wand holding a Greek spell, everything is kept. Both behaviors are toggleable.

[h2]Settings[/h2]
All settings are in the mod settings menu and apply immediately, no restart:
[list]
[*][b]Wand Structure Panel[/b] — the text tree on/off
[*][b]Wand Structure Panel: Text Size[/b] — Tiny / Small / Medium / Large (forced to Large with a font mod or a non-pixel-font language, see above)
[*][b]Slot Brackets[/b] — the in-UI rainbow brackets on/off
[*][b]Ignore Depleted Spells[/b] — leave 0-charge spells out of the structure
[*][b]Greek Wands: Keep Depleted Spells[/b] — the exception described above
[*][b]Debug Info (for bug reports)[/b] — resolution/GUI readout plus per-box guide lines
[/list]

[h2]Good to know[/h2]
[list]
[*]Purely visual. It reads your wands and draws on top of the UI — it changes no spells, wands, or world generation, and requests no unsafe Lua APIs. Safe to add to a run in progress, and safe to remove again.
[*][b]Shuffle wands show nothing at all[/b], on purpose. Their draw order re-randomizes every cycle, so any structure drawn would be one arrangement out of many — a confident-looking lie.
[*]The panel simulates the engine's real draw rules, read out of the game's own gun.lua. It can't know your mana, though, so a cast that fizzles part-way through may differ from what's shown.
[*]Spells whose effect depends on the game state at cast time — the Requirement ("If") spells and the random-draw spells — are marked [b]?[/b]; the structure shown is the condition-true / no-extras path.
[*]Mod-added spells aren't modeled structurally; they appear in the panel as plain leaves.
[/list]

[h2]Bugs, source, non-Steam installs[/h2]
If the brackets ever look misaligned, turn on [b]Debug Info (for bug reports)[/b], open your wand, and send the screenshot — it captures your resolution and the wand's measurements, which is exactly what's needed to fix it.

Source is MIT licensed on GitHub, along with a downloadable zip and install guide for anyone playing outside Steam:
[url=https://github.com/dontoisme/spell_bracket_visualizer]github.com/dontoisme/spell_bracket_visualizer[/url]
